#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "af1244b4-53fb-54ed-920b-6054a480b211" :question/math-academy-id "e-7884" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are $y = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y = \\pm 3 x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{b} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/id #uuid "9ddfab47-6101-5777-b4fa-780cc382cc25" :question/math-academy-id "e-7886" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ and $b = \\sqrt{25} = 5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "01791465-d1dc-5f4a-ac9a-72b822ab08b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7887" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157124 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "fc3042ef-d85e-54e2-8ea6-cd8570bcdd6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8055" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db0e8dbb-c5db-54e6-bad5-ce5488bccf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346327} {:answer/id #uuid "a61909ad-7bb4-5f82-9b8b-3750e2579bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346328} {:answer/id #uuid "16014427-57f8-522b-b2e0-7942df6e6430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346329} {:answer/id #uuid "c2935083-a9ca-5589-9c3b-f95aabe2b563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346330} {:answer/id #uuid "ca798256-17c8-5b28-aa33-9f2402690cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a61909ad-7bb4-5f82-9b8b-3750e2579bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346328} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ae907b9-6df9-547e-86a3-2e805cd0d3e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d04dc5b8-08a6-57d8-bded-d4e9790647ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106608" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=20?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{20} & =\\frac{20}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4134906b-ade5-585e-9eed-8a3ae86cf7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346334} {:answer/id #uuid "2daadef7-7b6d-5921-a1b7-07cb6e37173c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346335} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cc953a-b2c5-5cf1-a42e-eb152267c1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346336} {:answer/id #uuid "8be63e8e-43ea-5a46-bdfb-1cffe551e7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346337} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaf5efa-9ea0-5e87-a861-af9ff6bb9942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfaf5efa-9ea0-5e87-a861-af9ff6bb9942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346338} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f87579f-d2ff-556b-9b50-773ad175d759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fabfdbc4-b695-5ce4-bd18-c77c662dc09c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106615" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/09873582f6b52b28ca5223605479cc98df4ead7cf0940ea4cadfa66301694911.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a5a16f-5e2a-501c-9c05-7d8e3588051a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346341} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfbd48-73e4-5586-9b95-f706d1df1511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346342} {:answer/id #uuid "581020ee-018e-5525-a0a2-1b9c570127dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346343} {:answer/id #uuid "1afbf06a-297c-5ee2-b9d1-9b797167ac6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346344} {:answer/id #uuid "d162ca6b-2bb8-5578-b8c5-dd0c935d07e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a5a16f-5e2a-501c-9c05-7d8e3588051a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346341} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a424228-f16e-5293-ae53-ee24b73245ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fa09b3c-df1e-55fe-b2c4-e14f1cc9a066" :question/math-academy-id "q-106617" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/6044a47f621612d56138893be089f48a2d2a7060c13d044086154ab7892fac38.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0278f54d-318a-5abd-908b-a4eccdb8622e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346348} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f43754-8c54-53af-9947-b183e5faae90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "588169c4-5db7-5588-8399-debaa184ee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346350} {:answer/id #uuid "24880b86-4cae-5b70-a80e-144502a0e917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346351} {:answer/id #uuid "3efa7b90-80b1-5171-83e9-23663b93924d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=9\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0278f54d-318a-5abd-908b-a4eccdb8622e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346348} :answer-field/id #uuid "0325bf50-412d-5f7e-97f1-96cdf824d0c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc8cdff2-ffdf-55ed-95c0-18e8793dab2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106620" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/e2203c195dbcd9ae474f5d00f00e81d63ec6527c0367e96a8a7520522c967265.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-3)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21a5be1-6e5a-59c8-af9c-1a0b5dd3bc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346355} {:answer/id #uuid "538f8d72-22bf-5604-9112-99f278e54506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346356} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe8d6f1-4f0f-52db-a90e-ab67b237daa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346357} {:answer/id #uuid "b9786096-ec20-56cc-ba5a-a3c6aacf4893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4af1a68-b060-567a-ae00-36d818ee9615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe8d6f1-4f0f-52db-a90e-ab67b237daa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346357} :answer-field/id #uuid "a275f630-037e-5c2a-b416-95dfc6080918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90c35775-1aa2-582d-b3c4-a5b63abcbbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106626" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61998645-3741-53ab-8927-a497ce1cbd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346362} {:answer/id #uuid "7eceaa67-e4ff-52bc-9ade-65aa1f009f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346363} {:answer/id #uuid "952863ea-7c16-5ce0-8530-8271bc15b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346364} {:answer/id #uuid "4a625f29-b3b1-5f2f-a1e1-5e29b15a2f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346365} {:answer/id #uuid "6970ed7d-90dc-5363-a471-850dc615ecf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6970ed7d-90dc-5363-a471-850dc615ecf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346366} :answer-field/id #uuid "a321b7b9-3f1e-5e55-8686-a575a39afa87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1efecd9-c3e6-54d4-a6a0-6af38953777c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106671" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=8\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{8} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df3a649c-124d-535c-a595-7ddf961707a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346369} {:answer/id #uuid "9d44f8a8-171a-5f9a-b253-dea04c59a2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346370} {:answer/id #uuid "de94a279-5292-51ae-b81b-55109917d031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346371} {:answer/id #uuid "b5149c80-0510-5472-b9a6-0e2b784675fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346372} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2bee71-093b-5afb-8894-bc126721dc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5149c80-0510-5472-b9a6-0e2b784675fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346372} :answer-field/id #uuid "161b36d7-587e-5c40-a2b5-a00cf3315381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fe995fe-d9fb-57f1-81bc-8d7ca13c35e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106682" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ab11a3dde0aebbd4088c498e37da48909f9a309ca9d4ab29bfcfa788d53e5c5e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/f825ca9f9431ca38b14ed141639d5cf4189f0fde0526e943ddb956e112c3e4ab.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff92f23b-c5fc-5780-9d67-12a377e8284b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff92f23b-c5fc-5780-9d67-12a377e8284b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346376} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe5368bc-e1e1-50eb-9eac-2b5d739feafb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87358ea-65e8-5e8e-8430-96d83b5ed9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c87358ea-65e8-5e8e-8430-96d83b5ed9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346378} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71d719c-bae2-5673-af1c-680218afe7d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c73b5f1-13f8-5d58-9924-2732adfaffbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-275792" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/20138a518801a1bac0a76303a86d6ca4b2fe289f5dae230517730f7398de3945.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{4}{5}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,4)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=4.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{4}{5}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{b} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e3fb98d-0aa1-59f4-99f7-7dd5f4551cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{4}-\\frac{x^2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e3fb98d-0aa1-59f4-99f7-7dd5f4551cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{4}-\\frac{x^2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346381} :answer-field/id #uuid "218ac79c-d158-5f2b-a6e7-0600ffef1184" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ae69cc2-2076-5c1c-92eb-0c5855f63441" :question/math-academy-id "q-275794" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f75605a3-ee97-5813-b73a-d2ca30845098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f75605a3-ee97-5813-b73a-d2ca30845098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346384} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bd529c-07eb-5b2c-a903-1f6100fba042" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7df2b8ac-d3f5-5613-993f-e69a57f54d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-325806" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $b=\\sqrt{7}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78b2cc7-dc61-5dba-a08e-457a2b7b6a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78b2cc7-dc61-5dba-a08e-457a2b7b6a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346387} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e9a7de-9ac1-578a-abd7-725835ba3704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "201a3946-c922-53a0-bacb-39155ab1c2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-325818" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $5{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}=40$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d08964a-2b5d-5a7f-9f2c-99c4f3e02cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d08964a-2b5d-5a7f-9f2c-99c4f3e02cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346390} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe95b6e-5e04-5baa-8274-00296ef1886e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f683f444-563d-5b98-96a5-63779a59f5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-325821" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=20$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{20} & =\\frac{20}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb9f22c3-74c4-5b8e-bc81-08a88cfb90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9f22c3-74c4-5b8e-bc81-08a88cfb90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346393} :answer-field/id #uuid "872d4ecb-c1d8-53f2-87d5-d367cd2be66a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2117b3c-2428-544d-9df7-c0c99926ac4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2117b3c-2428-544d-9df7-c0c99926ac4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d84a12cf-ca2d-55a7-b2c1-bd05ace5fe9b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fab15ed8-d110-5c0a-bb37-2091717ed4f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-325849" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/e2c0bc639d03c94056b07ce1cc42976b88494f312e318199cef73043bc711c74.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,3)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee5f6e0-89ce-5954-98b4-0653b10a808a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{24}-\\frac{x^2}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346398} {:answer/id #uuid "f3958474-4370-572d-8659-d028ef6cac29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{24}+\\frac{x^2}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ee5f6e0-89ce-5954-98b4-0653b10a808a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{24}-\\frac{x^2}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346398} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b0f1d42-9a59-58e8-b31c-57af64087727" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea996889-9e23-5a96-b5ed-4f94b6d927c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-325905" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae80fb9-5c18-57b5-b4c9-82cebb514975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346402} {:answer/id #uuid "de67c31e-a563-5e0e-8ba9-590a5dd6ccad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae80fb9-5c18-57b5-b4c9-82cebb514975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346402} :answer-field/id #uuid "264d09be-01e6-51cb-8336-7f9cefe6c971" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5bd24f6-6d6e-535e-b32c-a9d04c9158b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5bd24f6-6d6e-535e-b32c-a9d04c9158b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346405} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b997d0-94ec-5723-916d-f0d7431121d4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41eceb87-fa62-57cc-85ca-797ce60afd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-326334" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/28c189efc805ec6aef299c4a89c1bb9299db7ad9f8544b86ed7509e6abc2e6a5.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5fd77d-d86f-52ca-803c-90632f9c4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 25\\sec t, y = 16\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156683} {:answer/id #uuid "dea51195-05a9-5077-9b8f-1cb5210ecbe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 16\\tan t, y = 25\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156684} {:answer/id #uuid "0756c206-2570-5d2b-a144-a0b47fb8ab2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 5\\tan t, y = 4\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156685} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbf47a-175f-53a6-83ec-593a67683c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 5\\sec t, y = 4\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156686} {:answer/id #uuid "45fb2299-8011-52f8-b3d5-997e406f2537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\tan t, y = 5\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45fb2299-8011-52f8-b3d5-997e406f2537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\tan t, y = 5\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e604fdc9-7e1f-58f2-89eb-02dfcad4d252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "181cf72e-dc08-50f6-a54a-4c45042abcc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-89068" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{25}=5$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d14abd88-19d5-5c27-96df-32a72cdf70da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346408} {:answer/id #uuid "837a9b6c-6611-596c-8389-edf7dc34f55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346409} {:answer/id #uuid "1f62084b-a4b4-5402-87e3-ec2d00ae180c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346410} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0479b9-a54d-553c-b2c2-d3325d7c74d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346411} {:answer/id #uuid "1243f2e4-c5e0-5795-b786-3dbb9a38ff49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad0479b9-a54d-553c-b2c2-d3325d7c74d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346411} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce7b458-b503-5443-b036-7fcee74b8332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f67d78d3-8bb9-5eaa-a448-9f4613ae0ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-89778" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc9ab26-c20e-5964-b619-80aab04f4e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [0, π], t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156863} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3fd94a-2d9e-5568-a52c-896ace185c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156864} {:answer/id #uuid "fe611f12-b578-556c-8d83-8582ecacc5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156865} {:answer/id #uuid "032f2c49-77a0-53ab-9bba-4be703482db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{3π}{2})∪(\\frac{3π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156866} {:answer/id #uuid "04b8bccc-5d46-5f5b-b488-e462f900369a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π, 0], t \\ne - \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe611f12-b578-556c-8d83-8582ecacc5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156865} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0c93a8-8a98-56cf-ab1e-f08a00b1e217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce7100e2-18bc-5970-9831-7deb51318ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90951" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/600981b59a97fd1d63c9da1f7ef45d58c8a2d5dcad1ec05df91a380e0f4e154e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/747613184fe04ecd53fb69e6e00c5d91152e571e9bc4db81adeef1063c057491.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2501f99-90b6-5a90-9b00-d0a071839134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346415} {:answer/id #uuid "215b54d5-ee02-5271-9669-ad4be828308d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346416} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c9a6e1-ebf2-5344-9823-1907ded81afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\pi ,0),\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346417} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6607c6-94e6-5756-ae9a-0a254d3bf75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346418} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd2afc3-8ad0-52fd-9616-d1f4e6630f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2501f99-90b6-5a90-9b00-d0a071839134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346415} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fcd964d-0e31-56ad-b64e-f9ba5be6135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29abe703-1b84-5d51-a4f6-f49a9a4dc183" :question/math-academy-id "q-91322" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/82bdf962acd0e4a9beb29c7f3cc4930e66e6e56f41f81ba944fd470edfdb2cd8.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ec38ac9d02fbc8ec8c4f79fccb36a0e91833e852127fd1a8c84e492276b8f95d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bacf7b85-1be6-512d-831b-0f1aa56dbbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156869} {:answer/id #uuid "bd839a41-3083-546a-b122-20649d4a0cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, 0]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156870} {:answer/id #uuid "10ba4d52-6b2d-5756-bfb3-d04bac120305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ (\\frac{π}{2}, π]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156871} {:answer/id #uuid "225f65d0-8f4d-56cb-bd93-71e1177331f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156872} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8a80f-777d-59f4-a2cf-655535777083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f8a80f-777d-59f4-a2cf-655535777083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156873} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c635ea-502b-5d7d-ace0-86168b760590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41da8a73-410b-5efd-98c6-b09b7edab8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-91323" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ebb0f765fca0bc4e3a0a789800779a51eb4173cf0384b8b3628ac679a5a3d692.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}]}
